  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nHigh pressure over the eastern Great Lakes early this morning\nwill drift overhead today into tonight before sliding east\noffshore on Sunday. This will bring fair weather today with\nhighs ranging from the upper 40s north to low 60s south. Clear\nskies and calm winds tonight will promote efficient radiational\ncooling tonight. Lows will drop into the 20s across the north\nand favorable radiational cooling spots south of the mountains.\nElsewhere, lows will be in the low to mid 30s with frost\npotential to the coast and across southern NH. Frost and Freeze\nheadlines have been issued for final night of the frost/freeze \nprogram across remaining active zones.\n\nWarmer and mostly dry weather is expected through much of\nSunday. Clouds will increase from SW to NE during the afternoon\nas moisture from the remnants of Isaias moves northeast into New\nEngland. Highs on Sunday will mainly be in the 60s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nModels remain in decent agreement that deep moisture from the\nremnants of Isaias will push into the Northeast Sunday and into\nthe forecast area late Sunday into Sunday night. Models also\ncontinue to suggest that a surface low will develop near\nsouthern New England Sunday night and will track ENE south of\nthe Gulf of Maine Monday. While measurable rainfall is likely\nsouth of the mountains late Sunday into Monday morning, the\nnorthward extent of measurable rainfall remains in question\nalong with total QPF. The last two runs of the NAM have been\nquite bullish bringing 1-2 inches along and south of the\nmountains while this seems to be an outlier compared to other \navailable guidance. The latest NBM brings around 0.5 inches \nacross southern NH with amounts decreasing to the north where \nareas north of the mountains see less than a tenth of an inch. \nThis seems reasonable given the spread amongst latest guidance.\n\nRain chances will diminish from north to south Monday morning\nwith the afternoon looking mostly dry with breaks in the cloud\ncover. It will be warm and almost feel a little humid with highs\nin the upper 60s and dew points in the upper 50s.\n\nThe next chance of rain arrives Monday night into Tuesday as a\ncold front drops south from Canada and a wave of low pressure\nforms along the front. Rain will become likely Monday night \ninto Tuesday morning across the mountains and points north while\ntiming of rain chances south of the mountains is variable \namongst available guidance. The latest NBM brings at least \nlikely PoPs across much of the area by mid morning Tuesday with \nPoPs increasing to 70-80 percent Tuesday afternoon. Rain chances\nwill continue into Tuesday night and linger into Wednesday\nmorning. Overall, this system looks to bring another round of\nbeneficial rainfall to the area. Colder air will work into the\narea Wednesday that will allow for lingering rain showers to\nchange to snow in the higher terrain of the White Mountains.\nCool and dry weather is favored the second half of next week\nunder high pressure.",
